WEBVTT

NOTE Module 08 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (133.8s). en.

1
00:00:00.176 --> 00:00:03.091
Welcome to Module Eight — natural language processing.

2
00:00:03.476 --> 00:00:09.722
The journey here is from raw text to tensors a network can learn from.

3
00:00:10.098 --> 00:00:12.731
We'll cover tokenization, embeddings, recurrent networks, and attention.

4
00:00:13.107 --> 00:00:16.408
First you have to turn text into numbers.

5
00:00:16.791 --> 00:00:19.976
Word-level tokens are simple but explode your vocabulary.

6
00:00:20.363 --> 00:00:23.898
Character-level keeps the vocabulary tiny but the sequences long.

7
00:00:24.271 --> 00:00:34.428
Subword schemes — BPE, WordPiece, SentencePiece — are the sweet spot, and modern language models live on roughly thirty to two-hundred thousand of them.

8
00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:38.840
But an integer ID carries no meaning, so we embed it.

9
00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:42.118
One-hot vectors are sparse and blind.

10
00:00:42.498 --> 00:00:49.237
Word2Vec learns from local co-occurrence with Skip-gram and CBOW; GloVe factorizes a global count matrix.

11
00:00:49.613 --> 00:01:01.822
The famous result — king minus man plus woman lands near queen — shows the geometry actually captures meaning, and pretrained embeddings give your downstream tasks a head start.

12
00:01:02.214 --> 00:01:08.079
To handle order, recurrent networks read a sequence one step at a time.

13
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:11.601
A SimpleRNN forgets, its gradient vanishing over long spans.

14
00:01:11.992 --> 00:01:20.115
The LSTM adds a gated cell that protects long-term state; the GRU does nearly as well with fewer parameters.

15
00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:24.914
Wrap either to read bidirectionally, and remember to mask your padding.

16
00:01:25.926 --> 00:01:28.703
Then comes the idea that changed everything: attention.

17
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:42.844
Compare a query against keys to get similarity scores, softmax them into weights, and take a weighted sum of the values — a context vector that decides where to look.

18
00:01:43.239 --> 00:01:52.004
Scale by the square root of d-k for stability, and run several heads in parallel to capture different relationships at once.

19
00:01:52.413 --> 00:02:02.887
Put it to work on real tasks — sentiment as sequence-to-class, named entity recognition at the token level, autoregressive text generation, and encoder–decoder translation.

20
00:02:03.263 --> 00:02:07.991
And in practice today, you'd start from BERT, GPT, or T5 weights.

21
00:02:08.364 --> 00:02:13.236
Let's build an attention model from scratch — open the labs.
